content.thinking:
There's a significant difference in the AI panel CSS:

**Old (committed) version:**
```css#safecast-ai-panel {  position: fixed;  top: 10px;
  left: -470px;    /* <-- starts off-screen to the LEFT */  width: 450px;  ...
}#safecast-ai-panel.open {  left: 10px;  /* <-- slides in from left */
}```

**New (current) version:**
```css#safecast-ai-panel {  position: fixed;  top: 10px;  right: -340px;  /* <-- starts off-screen to the RIGHT */  width: 320px;  ...  transition: right 0.3s ease;


}#safecast-ai-panel.open {  right: 0;  /* <-- slides in from right */
}```

The panel shifted from sliding in from the left side to the right side. Now I'm checking the toggle button positioning to see if it moved accordingly—the current version has it anchored to the bottom-right, and I need to verify if the old version had it positioned differently.

Looking at both versions, the toggle button was always on the right side at bottom-24px, so the panel position change to the right side was already committed in that earlier commit. The uncommitted changes added the map width adjustment and the class toggling logic, but those shouldn't be breaking the map display itself. Let me reconsider whether the issue is actually in the CSS or if something else is happening—maybe the local binary was built with these changes but the server is encountering an error when serving the map page.

The map template uses Go template syntax with variables like translations and coordinates, so if the HTML reformatting introduced any malformed template delimiters, the server would fail to parse and render the page. I should check the current file for any broken Go template syntax.

